  Over a 100 of them are defined in HTML's namespace#b to reproduce common layout practices, but since the </keywords>#b need to be repeated with a forward slash to end their effect, it quickly proves to be a rather cumbersome format to write directly. Notice also that each keyword introduced features we did not yet consider. To split lines, we were really only interested in "div"#b, the others instead changed the design#b or functionality#b of a segment, while of course providing their (semantic distinction)#b for computer recognition. For an image title as a link on a new line, should we write "<div><a><h1><svg/></h1></a></div>"#b? 

  Not separating these different concerns of editing becomes a recipe for confusion very quickly as you start layering their implicit, arbitrary and limited combinations inherent in keywords. To avoid this, the <span>#b keyword exhausts html's otherwise confusing effects in serving its only necessary one of (isolating content)#b. To display content that isn't text, we'll also remember <svg>#b, <canvas>#b, <video>#b and <audio>#b. 

  HTML's second feature, allowing us to control these other aspects separately, are field="value"#b pairs ("attributes"), included with keywords between their angle brackets: (<a href="https://jsinterface.org">)#b specifies the address of a link, (<svg width="500">)#b the dimensions of an image. Every keyword has several of them, still arbitrarily controlling design, functionality or semantics. Luckily, all also share one dedicated to each respectively: 
