(Layout)#h3{margin-left:.6em}

  To begin explicitly designing our text, we need the design language of "style" called ("cascading stylesheets")#b (css), consisting of semicolon-separated series of "field:value;"#b pairs. Its field to control linebreaks is actually called display#b, which we saw implicitly set from the default inline#b to block#b by <div>. Color#b made the font blue#b, font-weight#b made it bold#b, font-size#b scaled it to 2em#b ((em quad)@https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/em_quad). A title link is therefore simply: 
inference#script/highlight(` <span class="link" style="display:block;color:blue;font-weight:bold;font-size:2em;">Interface</span>`){max-width:100%;overflow:scroll}

  While easier to track, style rules can be long and repetitive. This prompts the second most important html element, the <style>#b tag which instead of displaying text, reads CSS rules between {accolades} after selectors#b referencing their elements by name#b, #id#b, .class#b or [attribute]#b, combinable with +#b (neighbor), ~#b (sibling), >#b (child), ( ){highlight:var(--text);} (descendant): 
inference#script/highlight(` <style>span.link{display:block;color:blue;font-weight:bold;font-size:2em;}</style>`){max-width:100%;overflow:scroll}

  This declutters a document well for us, but sets a distance between content and style, splitting our attention when editing one or the other. This is a problem of semantics, namely the lack of ("referential transparency")#b between CSS and HTML, especially with #id and .class being arbitrary custom references that may change on the elements (maybe not on a link, but for more complex elements), breaking their correspondence. Finding more constant selectors can alleviate this concern from the style's side. We can apply the semantics of the "a" tag with the "link"#b role, and reference it with attribute style selector instead. Since the stylesheet is read synchronously as part of the document, they should also be placed before the referenced elements to prevent a 'flash of unstyled content' (FOUC) before they load:
inference#script/highlight(` <style>span[role=link]{display:block;color:blue;font-weight:bold;font-size:2em;}</style>
 <span role="link">Interface</span>
`){max-width:100%;overflow:scroll}

  This leaves style rules meaningful enough even in isolation, that we can move them as far as to a separate file from the content: 

inference#script/highlight(` /* /style */
 span[role=link]{display:block;color:blue;font-weight:bold;font-size:2em;}`){max-width:90%;overflow:scroll;width:90%;margin:auto;border-radius:.5em; border:2px dashed #616161}

  A third element in HTML exists to "link" such adjacent files to a document as modules#b, provided their (Multipurpose Internet Mail Extension)@https://mimetype.io/all-types{"class":"invert reference","style":{"& *":{"filter":"invert(0.8)"}}} (mime) file format in its particular type#b field, along with href#b for address and rel#b to make this purpose explicit: 

inference#script/highlight(` <link href="./style" rel="stylesheet" type="text/css"/>`){max-width:100%;overflow:scroll}

  While this shows referential stability being under control, the second problem with distance is that referenced elements may not just change, but also disappear, making rules in a stylesheet obsolete which is only exacerbated by isolating them into modules. In addition, modules are loaded asynchronously, introducing the risk of FOUC. A second type of CSS statements called directives#b, starting with an @#b character, define operations or variables to use within the stylesheets like @import#b, @font-face#b, @keyframes#b (animations) and @media "query" (device dimension conditions), and including some that are loaded asynchronously anyway for which this won't matter. But at risk of trading distance back for redundancy, the @scope#b directive exists specifically to resolve referential transparency, when placing a stylesheet directly inside an element, enabling the corresponding :scope#b 'pseudo-class', a miscellaneous type of selectors to reference adjacent elements, in this case the one containing the stylesheet: 
