(Operation)#h3{margin-left:.6em}

  With layout and semantics under control, we can address functionality. Similar to style, action#b fields expose a language to customize interactions, but with much more freedom. HTML and CSS were only an imperative#b interface to pre-defined expressions, with little declarative#b capacity (selectors, "themes"; neither worth exploring far because of the base expression complexity). What makes a language declarative is its ability to assign custom semantics ((names)#b) to parts of its memory ((variables)#b) for reference elsewhere. Procedures#b are an extension of this ability to syntax itself, to direct its order of execution ((stack)#b), and dereference variables they declared ((scope)#b) as they conclude. 
  Functions#b are such declared procedures that can isolate variables ((arguments)#b) and conclude by yielding one. Javascript is a declarative, procedural and functional programming language with simple expressions#b that are mutually exclusive, and collectively exhaustive: 
{display:block;text-align:center;margin:.5em 10%;color:#616161}string#b, number#b, boolean#b, object#b, array#b, promise#b, generator#b, symbol#b, undefined#b, null#b, NaN#b, function#b, this#b, arguments#b
{text-align:left}  and operators#b: 
{display:block;text-align:center;margin:.5em 10%;color:#616161}= ((assignment)#b), ==, != ((equivalence)#b), ===, !== ((equality)#b), !, &&, ||, ?? ((logical)#b), +, -, *, /, %, <, >, <=, >=, ((arithmetic)#b), &, |, ^, ~, <<, >>, >>> ((bitwise)#b), import, export, let, var, const, if, else, do, while, for, in, of, try, catch, yield, return ((procedural)#b), (), {}, [], :, ?., ., ... (inference or ("destructuring")#b). 
{text-align:left}
  The browser itself in fact uses Javascript to process documents, reading every word, tag, attribute and style rule and translating them into pixels, semantics, design and (procedures)#b. Action fields provide an entry point for the latter to take over this mechanism at given events. Browsers implicitly include their values in a function declaration to receive two arguments from their (event listener)#b: a model of the containing element (as "this"#b) and the occurred event#b. To operate a link, we need to assign its procedure to a click#b action, along with its address#b to a data- field on the element: 
inference#script/highlight(` <span role="link" data-address="https://jsinterface.org" onclick="this.ownerDocument.defaultView.open(this.dataset.address,'_blank');">Interface</span>`){max-width:100%;overflow:scroll}

  Spelling out this procedure every time can again be redundant, so we can reach for the last important element, <script>#b, where we can write proper javascript to reference elements and declare functions in isolation. Like CSS rules, functions are also declared between {accolades} outside of an action field's context, but following their name and argument list between parentheses: 
function#script/highlight(
` function action(event){this.ownerDocument.defaultView.open(this.dataset.address,'_blank');}`){max-width:100%;overflow:scroll}
  In this case, the arguments will only include the event#b - the "this"#b argument where the element model is received is a special, default one to distinguish a peculiar type, the scope#b, from the context#b arguments. 
Its existence has a long history of misunderstanding, leading to a common, redundant classification of seemingly distinct function types: 
{text-align:left;margin-left:1em}
(1. free function)#b when it is undefined: 
free#script/highlight("action(...arguments)"){display:inline}
(2. closure)#b when it is explicitly assigned (bound) to a variable: 
closure#script/highlight("action.bind(scope)(...arguments)"){display:inline}
(3. method)#b when it is implicitly bound to a variable by being accessed as a "member" on it (typical of object-oriented programming): 
method#script/highlight("scope.action(...arguments)"){display:inline}
(4. functor)#b when it is dynamically passed along with free arguments: 
functor#script/highlight("action.call(...arguments)"){display:inline}.
{text-align:left}
  Before getting confused by this diversity, notice that the first three are all simply assuming a value for the scope argument, while a functor doesn't. This is generally called ("partial application")#b of a functor#b (pre-assigning some of its arguments), which in turn is only the mathematically accurate term ((category)#b) for functions between domains beyond arithmetic, such as spatial or structural types (which actually occur more in computer programs). What truly differentiates these functions then is rather their return type: operators#b return values, combinators#b return other functions. The "bind"#b method on Javascript functions is therefore simply a combinator returning the function in its scope with a scope of its own - be it an object or undefined: 
{text-align:left;margin-left:1em}
(1-3. partial application)#b: 
functor#script/highlight("action.bind.call(action,scope)(...arguments)"){display:inline}.
{text-align:left}
 Now calling the "call" method on this "bind" function seems superfluous: closure#script/highlight(`action.bind(scope)`){display:inline;white-space:normal;word-break:break-all} already implies "action" as its own scope - much like inference#script/highlight(`scope.action.call(scope,...arguments)`){display:inline;white-space:normal;word-break:break-all} is made unnecessary by this implicit binding of methods. The object-oriented paradigm has made this popular for reducing complexity in the short term, but it also adds an unnecessary dependency on membership. Should a function be compatible with multiple types, they all need to include it in their definition, "inherit" them from a more abstract class, or re-bind it from each other. Unfortunately, the Javascript base expressions listed above are littered with such unnecessary "method" bindings, achieved by all descending from actual abstract classes ("prototypes"): String, Number, Boolean, Array, Object, or in the current case Function: 

