  When you have more actions to assign, it is practical to group them in one object so you can derive them all in one lambda expression. This generalization makes this procedure independent#b from the action "namespace", allowing to further isolate (referentially transparent)#b modules with Javascript's export#b and import#b statements, offering a simplicity never possible with HTML and CSS. While selector specificity has helped us prevent mismatched content references in the isolation of CSS's declarative rules, this may still leave past or future elements out of scope in the case of JavaScript's procedural assignment of interactions. Luckily, browser actions don't need to be bound to an element in specific to receive events from it: when interactions happen, their event stores a reference to the ("target")#b element they originate in, and get ("propagated")#b to each containing element in the document. To make sure you receive them, every event can therefore be captured#b on a higher level, and be routed back to their target element from there. While you may choose the highest document level for this, it could imply a large set of independent actions to be stored on one object, risking disregard for bloat. A more modular approach is to reference actions in a data field on your elements, and capture only the ("load")#b event on the body tag, fired when all its elements are loaded, to perform these assignments. To capture events happening before the bindings are ready, make their listeners asynchronous to "buffer" their delegation: 

