</body>
</html>`){max-width:90%;overflow:scroll;width:90%;margin:auto;border-radius:.5em; border:2px dashed #616161}

  (The Myth of Frameworks)#h3{margin-left:.6em"}

  This triplet#b of an HTML document with CSS and JS modules is as convenient as it gets if you only have a static file editor and server. We're still dealing with three different languages tough, one more limited than the other, so (static site generation)#b (SSG) from a coherent fourth structure (shell, perl, C, python scripts) has been preferred since their inception. Two years into only HTML's existence, when dynamic content could only be generated by a server, static file servers ((httpd)#b, Apache#b, (Microsoft IIS)#b) adopted ("common gateway interface")#b (CGI) modules in 1993#b to execute such scripts. While the growing HTML namespace always needed preservation for backwards compatibility, Javascript#b and CSS#b were introduced in 1995 and 1996 to separate its concerns as we did above, distributing dynamic capabilities to the client side. Document design was still highly complicated in their early stages, so scripting practices matured into graphical interfaces like (Adobe Dreaweaver)#b (C++, 1997) and (Movable Type)#b (Perl, 2001), as the first commercially viable platforms. To keep the memory of CGI scripts available, dedicated servers were introduced for (server-side rendering (SSR))#b with Apache's (mod_php)#b (C, 1997), Tomcat#b (Java, 1999), Webrick#b (Ruby, 2000), Microsoft's ASP#b (C#, 2001), and WSGI#b (Python, 2003), each with a respective framework to control it: (PHP)#b, (Java Server Pages)#b, (Ruby on Rails)#b, ASP.NET#b and Django#b. Behind all the these brand names, the big idea they were all capitalizing on was to further bloat HTML in a fourth, heterogenous "template"#b format embedding CSS and/or JS (beauties like inference#script/highlight(`<%for item in $list%><span><%=$item%></span><%end%>`){display:inline;white-space:normal;word-break:break-all}), or at least introduce new "directives"#b (namespaces or just comments, eg. "server-side includes": ssi#fragment/document({"span":{"#text":"<!--#include virtual=\"./text\"-->"}}){font-family:monospace;color:#616161;word-break:break-all}). Good as this idea seems for many to this day, the even more elaborate graphical interfaces they produced in the form of ("Content Management Systems")#b ((Wordpress)#b 2003, OpenCms#b 2000, Shopify#b 2006, Sitecore#b 2001 and (django CMS)#b 2007) created an industry benefitting their maintainers more than its customers by subverting standards with their frameworks. 

  In the meantime, despite constant criticism for its volatility due to lack of traditional type annotations (which ruby and python also lack, just have less exposure as server-side languages), Javascript slowly evolved as the exclusive, ubiquitous programming language on web clients, where it proved uniquely accessable#b and intuitive#b. A new generation of amateur programmers emerged, and with them a path of least resistence towards abandoning complex server architectures in favor of (complex) (client-side rendering)#b, filling the web with static ("single page applications")#b devoid of content before their javascript re-executes for every client. By 2006 JQuery#b emerged as the first de facto (client-side framework)#b to challenge the dominance of PHP and Ruby, and this was only the inflexion point of javascript's popularity. To fight this dangerous trend, (static site generation)#b saw a rennaissance after 15 years in Jekyll#b (2008), a spinoff of Rails leveraging the new markdown@https://daringfireball.net/2004/03/introducing_markdown format for simplified (albeit limited) templating. 

  The dominance of javascript was decided in 2009#b with the arrival of Node.js#b: taking javascript's (V8 compiler)#b from the Chrome browser and augmenting it with operating system capabilities (file and network access) made javascript a language singularly capable of operating universally#b on both server and client sides. Its unique asynchronous runtime procedure inherited from its optimization to listen for browser events (the "event loop") made it more suitable for server operation than previous multi-threaded servers as well. With the new generation of professionals still enthralled by the ill-conceived prospects of client-side rendering, the opportunity of this JS singularity for dynamic dual-side rendering has come to be wasted on static site generation. 

  Backbone#b, Knockout#b and AngularJS#b were all released the same year to inaugurate the upcoming decade of race for most overengineered UI architecture ever, with as little progress made during as possible. Their redundant browser APIs ( backbone#script/highlight(`Backbone.Model.extend({}).on("change:field",event)`){display:inline;white-space:normal;word-break:break-all} ), witty ( knockout#script/highlight(`<span data-bind="template:{name:'item',data:item}">`){display:inline;white-space:normal;word-break:break-all} ) or less witty ( angular#script/highlight(`<span ng-repeat="item in items">{{item}}</span>`){display:inline;white-space:normal;word-break:break-all} ) html templates and Model-View-Controller/ViewModel (MVC/MVVM) alchemy inspired Ember#b to combine the worst of all worlds into an object-oriented hellscape in 2011. To represent the quiet subcurrent of reason, Meteor#b extracted the single substantial idea of applying the (observer pattern)#b from Knockout, and extended it for "local-first" synchronization with server sockets, still bundled alas with the bloat of a monolithic framework. 2012 was the year critics of JS at Microsoft launched their offensive with the release of the Typescript#b compiler, adding type annotation to the syntax without exposing a modular parser and compiler, creating a critical bottleneck between the industry and the language standards (which luckily resisted the lobby to integrate it). To deal with the proliferation of non-standard dialects of javacript, general compilers such as Gulp, Eslint#b and Webpack#b (followed by Babel#b next year) became commonplace in 2013. While Hugo came out as another static site generator written in Go, the all-time champions of overengineering began to line up on a yearly basis: React#b, then Vue#b in 2014 and Angular#b in 2015 together took over the industry as the new standards of web development with their "component"#b architectures - a full detachment from the native document model using the ill-conceived object-oriented notion of nested (as opposed to sequential) composition. To React's credit, it left from for web standards, being reasonably modular to be used as a simple javascript library in isolation, but the profession was too gaslit by the complexity lobby to not depend on the Next.js#b framework promptly built around it in 2016. 

  To somewhat justify this dead end of technology and ensuing (market failure)@https://infrequently.org/2023/02/the-market-for-lemons/, even the consortium of web standards (W3C) was complicit in promoting (Web Components)@https://www.w3.org/TR/components-intro/, isolating styles and scripts internally in a "shadow dom" extension of the document model in 2014. Its ambitious showcase in Google's Polymer#b saw diminishing returns ever since as the concept isn't addressing any of the real problems with (concern separation)#b and (referential transparency)#b. To pave a way to redemption, the European Computer Manufacturers Association's Technical Committee 39 (ECMA TC39), responsible for the standard (Javascript language specification)@https://262.ecma-international.org/6.0/index.html (ECMA-262), introduced its module syntax in 2015. React took the opportunity to implement (server-side rendering)#b for its components, but it has barely been utilized outside of Next and Meteor's adoption internally, and "server components" to customize it took until 2020 to arrive. Anticipating modular JS, renown library author Rich Harris had them prioritized by his Rollup#b bundler in 2015, before returning to his static component framework Svelte#b in 2017, a html template to generate modular sites, with SvelteKit#b extending it for SSR in 2020. Combining both CSS and JS in HTML modules, its simplicity made it a strong contestant for static site generators like Gatsby from 2015 and 11ty from 2017, but React's JSX syntax imitating HTML has been a close second, with the former's explicit reliance on it, and several frameworks adopting what became known as the 'hyperscript' function signature ((fiber(name,attributes,children))#span{color:grey}) to allow it, from Mithril (since 2012) through HyperApp (2018) to Solid.js (2019). Given this popular atavism of HTML templates, Astro was able to merge Svelte, React and other frameworks in a unified html template in 2011, complete with SSR support from the start. 

  While frameworks have finally begun supporting dynamic servers, 15 years with Node.js has been wasted on static site generators and client-side rendering, and HTML remains the primary syntax being extended with never intended capabilities. After this painful history, it is past due to call frameworks a failure, and reclaim our confidence to program directly with JavaScript. 

