  Once this is declared, we can operate on it freely with JavaScript to "render" the syntax that browsers understand. While traversing the object, it would be strightforward to build a string to represent the html, but since we may want to use this function to render on client side too, we could also rely on browser methods to build its "document object model" (DOM) directly, and perhaps trigger actions programmatically. These methods are not available in Node.js by default, and are also quite complicated, so we will borrow an implementation called JSDOM by (Domenic Denicola)@https://domenic.me/ from the Web Hypertext Application Technology Working Group (WHATWG, responsible for the HTML standard since 2004 as the W3C's approach with XHTML fell out of favor). As also described in Interface@/Blik_2023_interface, to use dependencies less as a black box as with NPM, we can define its sources and get it bundled as a standard module. This can be a bit time consuming as build processes are not always so explicit, but it's available for JSDOM in Interface's source definitions (complete with its build scripts, exclusion of prettier integration, and edits to get circular imports asynchronously re-assigned in its bundle): 

